{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-21T14:17:05.435Z","sessionId":"2d4ac909-674d-4c60-84e6-9f60f265e8c3","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac4ee748b84ca5351</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_930e6d02db5347f38c89ce13</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-warner-openvela/2d4ac909-674d-4c60-84e6-9f60f265e8c3/tasks/ac4ee748b84ca5351.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Explore XSPI reference code\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Here is the detailed technical analysis of the XSPI flash driver implementation.\n\n---\n\n## XSPI Flash Driver Technical Summary\n\n### Files Examined\n\n- `/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/xspi.c` -- Main driver (600 lines)\n- `/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/xspi.h` -- Public header\n- `/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/boards/ATK_DNN647/board.c` -- Board-level VDD/power init\n- `/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/boards/ATK_DNN647/mpconfigboard.h` -- Board config (256Mbit flash)\n- `/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/boards/ATK_DNN647/pins.csv` -- Pin mappings\n- `/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/boards/NUCLEO_N657X0/board.c` -- Nucleo board init\n- `/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/boards/NUCLEO_N657X0/mpconfigboard.h` -- Nucleo config (512Mbit flash)\n- `/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/boards/NUCLEO_N657X0/pins.csv` -- Nucleo pin mappings\n\n---\n\n### 1. XSPI2 Peripheral Initialization Sequence\n\nThe initialization is implemented in `xspi_init()` at line 94 of `xspi.c`. The sequence is:\n\n**Step A -- Pin configuration (lines 96-107):** Eleven pins on GPIO port N (PN0 through PN11) are configured as XSPI2 Port 2 alternate function (AF9), with no pull and very-high speed:\n\n| Pin   | Function        | AF |\n|-------|-----------------|----|\n| PN0   | XSPIM_P2_DQS    | 9  |\n| PN1   | XSPIM_P2_CS     | 9  |\n| PN2   | XSPIM_P2_IO0    | 9  |\n| PN3   | XSPIM_P2_IO1    | 9  |\n| PN4   | XSPIM_P2_IO2    | 9  |\n| PN5   | XSPIM_P2_IO3    | 9  |\n| PN6   | XSPIM_P2_SCK    | 9  |\n| PN8   | XSPIM_P2_IO4    | 9  |\n| PN9   | XSPIM_P2_IO5    | 9  |\n| PN10  | XSPIM_P2_IO6    | 9  |\n| PN11  | XSPIM_P2_IO7    | 9  |\n\nNote: PN7 and PN12 are skipped -- they are not part of the XSPI2 port 2 pin set.\n\n**Step B -- Clock and reset (lines 108-123):** A force-reset then release-reset cycle is applied to all four XSPI-related peripherals (XSPIM, XSPI1, XSPI2, XSPI3) via the AHB5 bus. Clock sources are set to HCLK for both XSPI1 and XSPI2. Clocks are then enabled for XSPIM, XSPI1, and XSPI2.\n\n**Step C -- XSPIM direct mode (lines 126-128):** Both XSPI1 and XSPI2 are disabled, then `XSPIM-&gt;CR` is cleared to 0 to select direct (non-multiplexed) mode.\n\n**Step D -- XSPIx CR (Control Register, lines 132-141):**\n- `FTHRES = 3` -- FIFO threshold at 4 bytes\n- `MSEL = 0` -- FLASH 0 selected\n- `CSSEL = 0` -- NCS1 used as chip select\n- `DMM = 0` -- Dual-memory mode disabled\n- `TCEN = 1` -- Timeout counter enabled\n- `DMAEN = 0` -- DMA disabled\n- `EN = 0` -- Peripheral starts disabled\n\n**Step E -- DCR1 (Device Configuration Register 1, lines 143-149):**\n- `MTYP = 1` -- Macronix memory type\n- `DEVSIZE = (FLASH_SIZE_BITS_LOG2 - 4)` -- For the ATK-DNN647 with 256Mbit (2^28), this is `28 - 3 - 1 = 24`. For the Nucleo with 512Mbit (2^29), it is `29 - 3 - 1 = 25`.\n- `CSHT = (CS_HIGH_CYCLES - 1)` -- Default 1 (2 high cycles minus 1)\n- `FRCK = 0` -- CLK is not free-running\n- `CKMODE = 0` -- CLK idles low\n\n**Step F -- DCR2 (lines 151-154):**\n- `PRESCALER = (MICROPY_HW_XSPI_PRESCALER - 1)` -- Default 3, giving `F_CLK = F_AHB / 4`\n\n**Step G -- DCR3, DCR4 (lines 156-163):** Zeroed. Refresh is disabled (non-volatile memory).\n\n**Step H -- TCR (lines 165):** Zeroed (no dummy cycles in the default configuration).\n\n**Step I -- Enable sequence (lines 168-174):** XSPIx is enabled, then the XSPIM is reconfigured (all XSPI peripherals disabled, XSPIM CR cleared, then XSPIx re-enabled). This double-enable pattern appears to be required to properly synchronize the XSPIM with the individual XSPI peripheral.\n\n**Step J -- Flash hardware reset (lines 176-183):** If the board defines `pyb_pin_FLASH_RESET`, the external flash is hardware-reset via a GPIO toggle: low for ~1ms, then high for ~10ms (delays calibrated for ~800MHz CPU). This ensures the flash returns to standard SPI mode.\n\n**Step K -- Mode selection and memory mapping (lines 187-193):** The driver calls `xspi_switch_to_dtr()` followed by `xspi_memory_map_888()` to enter Octal DTR mode with memory mapping. An `if(1)/else` construct allows falling back to SPI-only (`xspi_memory_map_111()`) if changed to `if(0)`.\n\n---\n\n### 2. SPI-to-Octal DTR (DOPI) Mode Switch\n\nThe mode transition is in `xspi_switch_to_dtr()` at line 444 of `xspi.c`. This implements the Macronix MX25UM-series DOPI entry procedure:\n\n**Step 1 -- Write Enable (WREN, 0x06):** Sent as a single-line SPI command with no address and no data (line 448).\n\n**Step 2 -- Poll WEL bit (lines 451-456):** Read status register 0x05 in SPI mode, polling for bit 1 (WEL = Write Enable Latch). Retries up to 100 times.\n\n**Step 3 -- Write Configuration Register 2 (WRCR2, 0x72) at address 0x00000000 (line 459-460):** A single byte value of `0x02` is written. This sets bit 1 of CR2, which switches the flash into Octal DTR (DOPI) mode per the Macronix register map.\n\n**Step 4 -- Mark DTR enabled (line 462):** The `xspi_dtr_enabled` flag is set to `true`.\n\nThe reverse procedure `xspi_switch_to_spi()` (line 465) sends a DTR-format WREN (`0x06f9`) followed by a DTR-format WRCR2 (`0x728d`) with 2 bytes of zero to clear the DOPI bit.\n\n**DTR command encoding note:** All DTR commands use 16-bit opcodes where the second byte is the bitwise complement of the first (e.g., `0x06`/`0xf9`, `0x72`/`0x8d`, `0x12`/`0xed`). This is the standard Macronix Octal DTR protocol.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Memory-Mapped XIP Configuration\n\nTwo memory-mapped configurations are provided:\n\n**SPI mode -- `xspi_memory_map_111()` (line 394):**\n- CCR: 1-line data, 32-bit address (1-line), 1-line instruction\n- TCR: 0 dummy cycles\n- IR: `0x13` (READ4B -- 4-byte-address read command)\n- LPTR: 1024 CLK cycles (timeout for auto-idle detection)\n- FMODE: 3 (memory-mapped mode)\n\n**Octal DTR mode -- `xspi_memory_map_888()` (line 410):**\n- CCR: DQS enabled, DTR on all phases, 8-line data, 8-line address, 8-line instruction, 16-bit instruction size\n- TCR: **20 dummy cycles** (minimum for the flash to present data; the flash may insert more by holding DQS low)\n- IR: `0xee11` (8DTRD -- Octal DTR read command, 16-bit with complement)\n- LPTR: 1024 CLK cycles\n- FMODE: 3 (memory-mapped mode)\n\n**XIP base addresses (defined in the xspi_flash_t structs at lines 57-65):**\n- XSPI1: `0x90000000`\n- XSPI2: `0x70000000`\n\nThe driver currently hard-codes `XSPIx = XSPI2` (line 47), so the effective XIP base is `0x70000000`.\n\n**Memory map exit (`xspi_memory_map_exit()`, line 431):** If the peripheral is busy (a transfer is ongoing), it aborts by setting `XSPI_CR_ABORT`, waits for TCF, clears the flag, then waits for BUSY to deassert. FMODE is then set to 0 (indirect write mode).\n\n**Cache invalidation (line 509):** On `MP_QSPI_IOCTL_MEMORY_MODIFIED`, both the I-cache and D-cache are invalidated for the affected address range via `SCB_InvalidateICache_by_Addr` and `SCB_InvalidateDCache_by_Addr`.\n\n---\n\n### 4. VDDIO3 Power Domain Configuration\n\nThe VDDIO3 configuration is identical in both board files (`board.c` for ATK_DNN647 and NUCLEO_N657X0). It is implemented in `board_config_vdd()`.\n\n**Clocks enabled (lines 40-43):**\n- `LL_AHB4_GRP1_PERIPH_PWR` -- Power control\n- `LL_APB4_GRP2_PERIPH_BSEC` -- Boot security (OTP fuses)\n- `LL_APB4_GRP2_PERIPH_SYSCFG` -- System configuration (IO compensation)\n\n**VDDIO3 enable and voltage selection (lines 68-72):**\n1. `LL_PWR_EnableVddIO3()` -- Powers up the VDDIO3 domain\n2. If the BSEC HSLV fuse is programmed: `LL_PWR_SetVddIO3VoltageRange(LL_PWR_VDDIO_VOLTAGE_RANGE_1V8)` -- selects 1.8V mode (required for high-speed Octal DTR operation)\n3. `SYSCFG-&gt;VDDIO3CCCR |= SYSCFG_VDDIO3CCCR_EN` -- enables IO compensation cell for VDDIO3 pins\n\n**Other power domains configured in the same function:**\n- VDDIO2: Enabled, 3.3V range, compensation enabled\n- VDDIO4: Enabled, 3.3V range, compensation enabled\n- VDDADC: Enabled (for ADC)\n- VDDUSB: Enabled (for USB)\n\n**Standby recovery (`board_leave_standby()`):** Re-enables all VDDIO domains and their compensation cells. For VDDIO3, the voltage range selection is described as \"retained\" (the OTP fuse setting persists through standby), so only the enable and compensation are re-done.\n\n**ATK-DNN647 extended init:** The ATK-DNN647 `board_early_init()` additionally configures SMPS supply (`HAL_PWREx_ConfigSupply(PWR_SMPS_SUPPLY)`), voltage scaling scale 0 (`HAL_PWREx_ControlVoltageScaling(PWR_REGULATOR_VOLTAGE_SCALE0)`), and pre-configures PLL2 and PLL4 for the DCMIPP/CSI camera interface. When running from external flash (`MICROPY_HW_RUNS_FROM_EXT_FLASH`), the VDDIO domains are re-enabled in `board_early_init()` since mboot already did the initial configuration.\n\n---\n\n### 5. BSEC OTP Fuse Programming for High-Speed IO\n\nThe BSEC (Boot Security) OTP fuse configuration is in `board_config_vdd()`, lines 44-57 of both board files.\n\n**Fuse identity:** OTP word 124 (`BSEC_HW_CONFIG_ID = 124U`), bit 15 (`BSEC_HWS_HSLV_VDDIO3 = 1U &lt;&lt; 15`). Per RM0486 Section 5, Table 18, this bit enables the High-Speed Low-Voltage (HSLV) optimization for the VDDIO3 domain.\n\n**Programming sequence:**\n1. Read OTP word 124 via `HAL_BSEC_OTP_Read()`.\n2. If the read fails, set `fuse = 0` (assume unprogrammed).\n3. If the read succeeds but bit 15 is not set, program the fuse via `HAL_BSEC_OTP_Program()` with `HAL_BSEC_NORMAL_PROG` mode, setting bit 15 while preserving existing bits (`fuse | mask`).\n4. Read back the fuse value to confirm programming succeeded.\n5. If any step fails, `fuse` is set to 0, and VDDIO3 will remain at the default 3.3V mode (the high-speed IO optimization will not be activated).\n\n**Why this matters:** The Macronix MX25UM-series flash in Octal DTR mode operates at high clock speeds that require the 1.8V IO voltage on the VDDIO3 domain. The HSLV OTP fuse is a one-time-programmable (irreversible) setting that tells the STM32N6 silicon to enable optimized IO drivers for low-voltage high-speed signaling on the VDDIO3 pins (port N, where XSPI2 pins reside). Without this fuse, `LL_PWR_SetVddIO3VoltageRange(LL_PWR_VDDIO_VOLTAGE_RANGE_1V8)` cannot be called, and the IO pads would be configured for 3.3V, which is incompatible with the flash operating in DOPI mode at high frequency.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Additional Implementation Details\n\n**QSPI protocol translation layer:** The driver presents itself as a `mp_qspi_proto_t` (line 590) and translates standard SPI commands into either 1-line or 8-line DTR equivalents depending on the current mode. Key command translations when in DTR mode:\n\n| SPI Command | DTR 16-bit Opcode | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| WREN (0x06) | 0x06f9 | Write enable |\n| RDSR (0x05) | 0x05fa | Read status, 4 dummy cycles, result is 2-byte DTR |\n| RDID (0x9F) | 0x9f60 | Read device ID (noted as not fully working -- result is STR format) |\n| PP 4B (0x12) | 0x12ed | Page program, 32-bit address |\n| SE 4B (0x21) | 0x21de | Sector erase, 32-bit address |\n| SLEEP (0xB9) | 0xb946 | Deep power-down |\n| AWAKE (0xAB) | 0xab54 | Release from deep power-down |\n\n**24-to-32-bit address promotion (lines 544-549):** SPI commands using 24-bit addresses (WRITE 0x02, SEC_ERASE 0x20) are automatically promoted to their 32-bit equivalents (0x12, 0x21) before being dispatched.\n\n**FIFO access:** Data is transferred one byte at a time through the DR register. An untested 4-byte-at-a-time path exists (lines 220-232) but is disabled behind `#if 0`. The FIFO level is polled via `SR.FLEVEL` for reads and `SR.FTF` for writes, with timeout-error checking (`SR.TEF`) on each iteration.\n\n**Flash devices supported:**\n- ATK-DNN647: MX25UM25645G (256Mbit / 32MB), `XSPIFLASH_SIZE_BITS_LOG2 = 28`\n- NUCLEO-N657X0: MX25UM51245GXDI00 (512Mbit / 64MB), `XSPIFLASH_SIZE_BITS_LOG2 = 29`\n\n**Block device partitioning:**\n- ATK-DNN647: 4MB offset, 4MB usable filesystem out of 32MB total\n- NUCLEO-N657X0: 4MB offset, 60MB usable filesystem out of 64MB total\n\n**Bootloader (mboot) integration:** Both boards define `MBOOT_BOARD_EARLY_INIT` to call `mboot_board_early_init()`, which calls `board_config_vdd()` followed by `xspi_init()`. The mboot SPI flash layout string uses the XIP base address `0x70000000`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>29258</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>11</tool_uses><duration_ms>94813</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
