ead fails, set `fuse = 0` (assume unprogrammed).\n3. If the read succeeds but bit 15 is not set, program the fuse via `HAL_BSEC_OTP_Program()` with `HAL_BSEC_NORMAL_PROG` mode, setting bit 15 while preserving existing bits (`fuse | mask`).\n4. Read back the fuse value to confirm programming succeeded.\n5. If any step fails, `fuse` is set to 0, and VDDIO3 will remain at the default 3.3V mode (the high-speed IO optimization will not be activated).\n\n**Why this matters:** The Macronix MX25UM-series flash in Octal DTR mode operates at high clock speeds that require the 1.8V IO voltage on the VDDIO3 domain. The HSLV OTP fuse is a one-time-programmable (irreversible) setting that tells the STM32N6 silicon to enable optimized IO drivers for low-voltage high-speed signaling on the VDDIO3 pins (port N, where XSPI2 pins reside). Without this fuse, `LL_PWR_SetVddIO3VoltageRange(LL_PWR_VDDIO_VOLTAGE_RANGE_1V8)` cannot be called, and the IO pads would be configured for 3.3V, which is incompatible with the flash operating in DOPI mode at high frequency.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Additional Implementation Details\n\n**QSPI protocol translation layer:** The driver presents itself as a `mp_qspi_proto_t` (line 590) and translates standard SPI commands into either 1-line or 8-line DTR equivalents depending on the current mode. Key command translations when in DTR mode:\n\n| SPI Command | DTR 16-bit Opcode | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| WREN (0x06) | 0x06f9 | Write enable |\n| RDSR (0x05) | 0x05fa | Read status, 4 dummy cycles, result is 2-byte DTR |\n| RDID (0x9F) | 0x9f60 | Read device ID (noted as not fully working -- result is STR format) |\n| PP 4B (0x12) | 0x12ed | Page program, 32-bit address |\n| SE 4B (0x21) | 0x21de | Sector erase, 32-bit address |\n| SLEEP (0xB9) | 0xb946 | Deep power-down |\n| AWAKE (0xAB) | 0xab54 | Release from deep power-down |\n\n**24-to-32-bit address promotion (lines 544-549):** SPI commands using 24-bit addresses (WRITE 0x02, SEC_ERASE 0x20) are automatically p