start.c` / `stm32_start.h` | Boot sequence (naked `__start` clears MSPLIM/PSPLIM, then `__start_c` handles VTOR, FPU, BSS, clock config, SYSCFG, VDDIO, lowputc, nx_start) |\n| `stm32_idle.c` | Idle thread implementation |\n| `stm32_timerisr.c` | SysTick timer interrupt |\n| `Make.defs` / `CMakeLists.txt` | Build system integration |\n\n**What is implemented:** GPIO, USART1, PLL1 clock tree, power domain init, interrupt controller, boot/startup, SysTick, low-level serial.\n\n### 2. HARDWARE REGISTER DEFINITIONS\n\n**Location:** `/home/warner/openvela/nuttx/arch/arm/src/stm32n6/hardware/`\n\n| File | Contents |\n|------|----------|\n| `stm32n6xxx_memorymap.h` | Full peripheral base address map |\n| `stm32n6xxx_gpio.h` | GPIO register offsets and bitfield definitions (MODER, OTYPER, OSPEED, PUPDR, IDR, ODR, BSRR, AFR, SECCFGR, etc.) for ports A-H, N-Q |\n| `stm32n6xxx_pinmap.h` | Alternate function pin definitions (only USART1 TX/RX on PE5/PE6 AF7) |\n| `stm32n6xxx_rcc.h` | RCC register offsets and bitfield definitions (CR, SR, CFGR1/2, PLL1CFGR1/3, IC1/2/3/6/11 CFGR, DIVENR, MEMENR, AHB4ENR, APB1LENR, APB2ENR, APB4HENR, CCIPR13, sleep-mode registers) |\n| `stm32n6xxx_pwr.h` | PWR register definitions (CR1-4, VOSCR, BDCR1/2, DBPCR, CPUCR, SVMCR1/2/3) |\n| `stm32n6xxx_uart.h` | USART register definitions (CR1/2/3, BRR, ISR, ICR, RDR, TDR, etc.) |\n| `stm32n6xxx_syscfg.h` | SYSCFG register definitions (INITSVTORCR, VDDIO2CCCR, VDDIO3CCCR, VDDCCCR) including ES0620 I/O compensation mitigation |\n\n### 3. KCONFIG CONFIGURATION\n\n**Chip-level Kconfig** (`/home/warner/openvela/nuttx/arch/arm/src/stm32n6/Kconfig`):\n- Defines `STM32_N6_PERIPHERALS` (auto-selects `STM32_HAVE_USART1`)\n- Defines `STM32_STM32N6XXXX` \n- Single chip choice: `ARCH_CHIP_STM32N657X0`\n- No XSPI, no DMA, no SPI, no I2C, no timer configs -- very minimal\n\n**Arch-level Kconfig** (`/home/warner/openvela/nuttx/arch/arm/Kconfig` line 523):\n- `ARCH_CHIP_STM32N6` selects Cortex-M55 architecture\n\n**Board Kconfig** (`/home/warner/openvela/nuttx/boards/arm/stm32n6/nucleo-n657x0-q/Kconfig`):\n- Empty placeholder -- no board-specific options\n\n### 4. GPIO PIN CONFIGURATION SYSTEM\n\n**`stm32_gpio.h`** provides a 20-bit encoding scheme:\n- Bits 18-19: Mode (INPUT/OUTPUT/ALT/ANALOG)\n- Bits 16-17: Pull-up/down (FLOAT/PULLUP/PULLDOWN)\n- Bits 12-15: Alternate function (AF0-AF15)\n- Bits 10-11: Speed (2MHz/25MHz/50MHz/100MHz)\n- Bit 9: Open-drain/Push-pull\n- Bit 8: Output value / EXTI\n- Bits 4-7: Port (A=0..H=7, N=8, O=9, P=10, Q=11)\n- Bits 0-3: Pin (0-15)\n\n**`stm32_gpio.c`** implements `stm32_configgpio()`, `stm32_unconfiggpio()`, `stm32_gpiowrite()`, `stm32_gpioread()` -- all fully functional with 12 GPIO ports.\n\nThe GPIO system is complete and ready for XSPI pin mux configuration. XSPI pins would simply use `GPIO_ALT | GPIO_AF&lt;n&gt; | GPIO_SPEED_100MHZ | GPIO_PUSHPULL | GPIO_PORTx | GPIO_PINy` encoding.\n\n### 5. CLOCK AND RCC CONFIGURATION\n\n**Clock tree** (from `board.h`):\n- HSI 64 MHz -&gt; PLL1 (M=4, N=50) -&gt; 800 MHz VCO\n- IC1 /4 = 200 MHz -&gt; CPU clock\n- IC2 /8 = 100 MHz -&gt; SYSCLK\n- IC3 /32 = 25 MHz -&gt; **XSPI2 kernel clock (reserved)** -- comment says \"reserved\"\n- IC6 /12 = 66.7 MHz -&gt; SYSCLK\n- IC11 /8 = 100 MHz -&gt; SYSCLK\n- HPRE /2 = 50 MHz -&gt; HCLK\n\n**Key observation in `stm32n6xx_rcc.c` line 207:**\n```c\n *   IC3  = VCO / (IC1_DIV * 4) -&gt; XSPI2 kernel clock (reserved)\n```\nIC3 is already configured but described as \"reserved\" for XSPI2. This is a head start -- the clock source for XSPI2 is already being set up, but there is no code to actually enable XSPI peripheral clocks.\n\n### 6. WHAT EXISTS FOR XSPI (Partial/Minimal)\n\nThe following XSPI-related items already exist:\n\n**In `stm32n6xxx_memorymap.h`:**\n- `STM32_XSPI1_BANK  0x90000000` -- XSPI1 memory-mapped region\n- `STM32_XSPI2_BANK  0x70000000` -- XSPI2 memory-mapped region\n- `STM32_XSPI2_BASE  0x5802A000` -- XSPI2 controller register base (AHB5, Secure)\n- `STM32_XSPIM_BASE  0x5802B400` -- XSPIM IO Manager register base (AHB5, Secure)\n\n**In `stm32n6xx_irq.h`:**\n- `STM32_IRQ_XSPI1` = IRQ 186 (FIRST+170)\n- `STM32_IRQ_XSPI2` = IRQ 187 (FIRST+171)\n- `STM32_IRQ_XSPI3` = IRQ 188 (FIRST+172)\n\n**In `stm32n6xx_rcc.c`:**\n- IC3 divider is configured as `VCO / (IC1_DIV * 4)` = 25 MHz, documented as \"XSPI2 kernel clock (reserved)\"\n\n**In `arch/arm/include/stm32n6/chip.h`:**\n- Comment mentions: \"Code runs from the on-chip AXI SRAM (loaded by the debugger in DEV mode, or by an FSBL from external XSPI flash in normal boot).\"\n\n### 7. WHAT IS MISSING FOR XSPI SUPPORT\n\nThe following components need to be created:\n\n**A. Missing Base Addresses (`stm32n6xxx_memorymap.h`):**\n- `STM32_XSPI1_BASE` -- XSPI1 controller register base is not defined (only XSPI2 and XSPIM are)\n- `STM32_XSPI3_BASE` -- XSPI3 controller register base is not defined\n- `STM32_HPDMA2_BASE` -- HPDMA2 base address (needed if XSPI uses HPDMA2 for data transfers)\n\n**B. Missing Hardware Register Definitions (new header needed):**\n- `hardware/stm32n6xxx_xspi.h` -- XSPI register offsets and bitfield definitions. This would include:\n  - XSPI control registers (CR, DCR1/2, TCR, IR, ABR, LPRT, WCCR, WTCR, etc.)\n  - XSPI status registers (SR, FCR)\n  - XSPI data registers (DLR, DR)\n  - XSPI communication configuration registers\n  - Memory-mapped mode configuration registers\n  - XSPIM (IO Manager) register definitions for port assignment\n\n**C. Missing RCC Clock Enable (`stm32n6xxx_rcc.h` and `stm32n6xx_rcc.c`):**\n- No `RCC_AHB5ENR_XSPI2EN` (or equivalent) bit definition for enabling the XSPI2 peripheral clock\n- No `RCC_AHB5ENR` register offset defined at all (the AHB5 bus enable register is absent from `stm32n6xxx_rcc.h`)\n- `stm32n6xx_rcc.c` does not enable any AHB5 peripheral clocks -- only AHB4 (GPIO/PWR) and APB2 (USART1) are handled in `stm32_rcc_enableperipherals()`\n- Need to add AHB5 clock enable for XSPI2 (and potentially XSPI1, HPDMA1/2)\n\n**D. Missing XSPI Driver Source (new files needed):**\n- `stm32_xspi.h` -- XSPI driver API header (init, read, write, memory-mapped mode, command sequences)\n- `stm32_xspi.c` -- XSPI driver implementation\n- This would be the main driver handling XSPI controller initialization, command/address/data phases, and optionally memory-mapped mode for execute-in-place (XIP)\n\n**E. Missing Kconfig Options (`stm32n6/Kconfig`):**\n- `STM32_XSPI1` / `STM32_XSPI2` / `STM32_XSPI3` -- enable/disable individual XSPI instances\n- `STM32_XSPI_DMASUPPORT` -- DMA transfer support\n- `STM32_XSPI_MEMMAP` -- memory-mapped mode support\n- `STM32_HAVE_XSPI` -- capability flag\n- Related DMA, clock source, and frequency options\n\n**F. Missing Pin Definitions (`stm32n6xxx_pinmap.h`):**\n- No XSPI GPIO alternate function pin definitions. Need to add pin mappings for XSPI1 and XSPI2 pins (CLK, DQS, D0-D7, CS, etc.) based on the STM32N657 datasheet alternate function table. For XSPI2 on the Nucleo board, these would typically be on specific ports with specific AF numbers.\n\n**G. Missing Board-Level XSPI Configuration (`board.h`):**\n- No `GPIO_XSPI2_*` pin selection macros\n- No XSPI clock configuration defines\n- No XSPI flash device configuration (size, page size, sector size, etc.)\n\n**H. Missing Board Source Integration (`stm32_bringup.c`):**\n- No XSPI initialization or flash registration in the bringup path\n- No MTD device registration for external flash\n\n**I. Missing Linker Script Support (`flash.ld`):**\n- The current linker script only defines a single SRAM region at 0x34000400. For XSPI flash support, it would need optional regions for XSPI memory-mapped areas (0x90000000 for XSPI1, 0x70000000 for XSPI2) if XIP is desired.\n\n**J. Missing Build System Integration (`Make.defs` / `CMakeLists.txt`):**\n- `stm32_xspi.c` needs to be conditionally added to both `Make.defs` and `CMakeLists.txt` based on `CONFIG_STM32_XSPI1` or `CONFIG_STM32_XSPI2`\n\n**K. Missing NuttX Generic XSPI/MTD Infrastructure:**\n- No generic XSPI bus driver exists in `drivers/` (the FlexSPI driver in imxrt is i.MX-specific)\n- The existing `drivers/spi/qspi_flash.c` provides a QSPI flash MTD framework that could potentially be adapted, but XSPI is a different (and more capable) peripheral than QSPI\n- May need a new `drivers/spi/xspi_flash.c` or similar, or a chip-specific MTD driver\n\n### 8. SUMMARY TABLE\n\n| Component | Status | Notes |\n|-----------|--------|-------|\n| Memory map (XSPI2_BASE, XSPIM_BASE) | PARTIAL | XSPI2 and XSPIM base addresses exist; XSPI1 and XSPI3 bases missing |\n| Memory-mapped regions (XSPI1/2_BANK) | EXISTS | Address regions 0x90000000 and 0x70000000 defined |\n| IRQ definitions (XSPI1/2/3) | EXISTS | IRQ numbers 186-188 defined |\n| RCC AHB5 clock enable | MISSING | No AHB5ENR register definitions, no XSPI clock enable bits |\n| XSPI register definitions | MISSING | No `stm32n6xxx_xspi.h` header |\n| XSPI driver | MISSING | No `stm32_xspi.c/h` |\n| XSPI Kconfig | MISSING | No XSPI-related Kconfig options |\n| XSPI pin mappings | MISSING | No GPIO alternate function definitions for XSPI pins |\n| Board XSPI configuration | MISSING | No XSPI setup in board.h, bringup.c, or defconfig |\n| IC3 clock (XSPI2 kernel) | RESERVED | Configured at 25 MHz but described as \"reserved\" |\n| DMA support for XSPI | MISSING | No HPDMA driver at all |\n| MTD/flash integration | MISSING | No flash-on-XSPI driver |\n| Generic XSPI bus framework | MISSING | Nothing in `drivers/` or `include/nuttx/` |</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>87943</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>67</tool_uses><duration_ms>170052</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
