{"parentUuid":"7e603bc2-ce12-4086-8438-a51152e0a4a9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_f4790c55e02f49c1bd042620","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"mimo-v2.5-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The error is \"failed to erase memory\". This could be because:\n\n1. The external flash loader might not be working correctly\n2. The XSPI pins might not be properly configured yet\n3. The flash might need special initialization before erase/writeLet me think about this... The STM32N6 has no internal flash, so the boot ROM loads code from external flash to SRAM. The external loader (.stldr) file needs to initialize the XSPI interface to communicate with the flash chip.\n\nLooking at the MicroPython reference, they use a different approach - they might use the ST-LINK to program the flash directly, or they might use a different method.\n\nActually, looking at the error more carefully, the connection succeeds (Device ID: 0x486, STM32N6xx, Cortex-M55), but the erase fails. This suggests the external loader can't communicate with the flash chip.\n\nPossible issues:\n1. The external loader might need the VDDIO3 to be configured first (1.8V for XSPI pins)\n2. The boot ROM might need to be in a specific state\n3. The flash chip might need specific initialization\n\nLet me check if there's a special sequence needed. Looking at the STM32N6 documentation and the MicroPython mboot implementation, I recall that:\n\n1. The STM32N6 boot ROM can load code from external flash automatically\n2. The external loader in STM32CubeProgrammer needs to initialize the XSPI interface\n3. There might be a need to configure the power domains first\n\nOne approach is to use the STM32CubeProgrammer with the \"Connect under reset\" option, or to use a different reset mode.\n\nAnother possibility is that the external loader file might not be compatible or might need additional configuration.\n\nLet me suggest some troubleshooting steps:\n1. Try with different reset mode (-rst instead of -swrst)\n2. Try connecting with \"connect under reset\"3. Check if the external loader is the correct one for the board\n4. Verify the flash chip is properly connected to the MCU\n\nActually, looking at the command output, I notice the connection succeeds after the 2nd attempt with8MHz frequency. This is normal. But the erase fails.\n\nLet me check if there's a specific sequence needed for the STM32N6. The issue might be that the XSPI pins are on VDDIO3 domain which needs to be configured for 1.8V operation before the external flash can be accessed.\n\nThe external loader should handle this, but maybe it doesn't. Let me suggest trying a different approach or checking if there's a pre-programmed FSBL that can be used to configure the power domains first.\n\nActually, I think the issue might be simpler - the external loader might need to be loaded differently, or the flash address might be wrong. Let me check the address - for XSPI2, the memory-mapped address is 0x70000000, but the actual flash programming might need a different address.\n\nLet me suggest some alternative approaches:\n1. Try using the STM32CubeProgrammer GUI to see if it provides more information\n2. Try using a different external loader if available\n3. Check if the board has any jumpers that need to be set for external flash access\n4. Try programming via UART/DFU mode instead of SWD\n\nI'll provide some troubleshooting suggestions to the user.","signature":""}],"usage":{"input_tokens":1173,"output_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":55552}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"38b0d20c-5aea-4846-acfb-0b9b35498caa","timestamp":"2026-08-21T15:34:42.967Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/warner/openvela","sessionId":"2d4ac909-674d-4c60-84e6-9f60f265e8c3","version":"2.1.237","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"synthetic-wandering-llama"}
