{"parentUuid":"23c82e16-11c5-4665-916a-6782b7a5d158","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_1e865b6a82634293a79a3ce2","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"mimo-v2.5-pro","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_58a380cebe5e44f298f00960","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/boards/NUCLEO_N657X0/board.md"}}],"usage":{"input_tokens":580,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":61952,"output_tokens":71,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"79ea3d7c-1278-4d5b-be56-3554a1a70911","timestamp":"2026-08-21T15:35:44.854Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/warner/openvela","sessionId":"2d4ac909-674d-4c60-84e6-9f60f265e8c3","version":"2.1.237","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"synthetic-wandering-llama"}
{"parentUuid":"79ea3d7c-1278-4d5b-be56-3554a1a70911","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"3f9b8bf6-0b96-4275-b359-ead8a0be8ffb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_58a380cebe5e44f298f00960","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tThe mboot bootloader must first be built and deployed to this board.  Make sure that\n2\tCN9 is in position 1-2 to select STLK as the 5V power source, that JP1 is in position\n3\t1-2 (lower position) and JP2 is in position 2-3 (upper position).  Then plug in a USB\n4\tcable into the ST-LINK port CN10.  This will allow mboot firmware to be programmed to\n5\tthe external SPI flash via ST's tools, eg:\n6\t\n7\t    make -C ports/stm32/mboot BOARD=NUCLEO_N657X0 deploy-trusted\n8\t\n9\tOnce mboot is installed, change CN9 to position 3-4 to select USB as the 5V power\n10\tsource, change JP2 back to position 1-2 (lower position) and change the USB cable to\n11\tCN8.  mboot will present a USB DFU device on this USB port, and the red LED2 should be\n12\tblinking at 1Hz to indicate that mboot is active.  If it's not active then hold the\n13\tUSER button and press NRST, and wait until all three LEDs are on, then release USER.\n14\tNow mboot will be active.\n15\t\n16\tOnce the USB DFU port can be seen, the firmware below can be programmed as usual with\n17\tany DFU loader.\n18\t\n19\t## Chip revision and signing header version\n20\t\n21\tThe STM32N657X0 chip is available in two silicon revisions, which require different\n22\tsigning header versions when building the trusted firmware image.  Check the revision\n23\tcode printed on the chip package:\n24\t\n25\t- **Rev code Z (Cut 1.1)**: use `STM32_N6_HEADER_VERSION = 2.1`\n26\t- **Rev code B (Cut 2.0)**: use `STM32_N6_HEADER_VERSION = 2.3` (default in\n27\t  `mpconfigboard.mk`)\n28\t\n29\tTo override the version without modifying `mpconfigboard.mk`, pass it on the make\n30\tcommand line:\n31\t\n32\t    make -C ports/stm32/mboot BOARD=NUCLEO_N657X0 STM32_N6_HEADER_VERSION=2.3\n33\t\n34\t## STM32CubeProgrammer v2.21.0 and later\n35\t\n36\tStarting with STM32CubeProgrammer v2.21.0, the signing tool no longer automatically\n37\taligns the binary payload to a 0x400-byte boundary.  The `--align` flag must be added\n38\texplicitly to the `STM32_SigningTool_CLI` invocation in both `ports/stm32/mboot/Makefile`\n39\tand `ports/stm32/Makefile`.  Find the line containing `STM32_SigningTool_CLI` in each\n40\tfile and append `--align`:\n41\t\n42\t    $(STM32_CUBE_PROGRAMMER)/bin/STM32_SigningTool_CLI -bin $^ -nk -of 0x80000000 -t fsbl -o $@ -hv $(STM32_N6_HEADER_VERSION) --align\n43\t\n44\tWithout this flag, mboot will not boot properly after programming.\n45\t\n46\t## Flashing mboot and firmware together via ST-LINK\n47\t\n48\tIf the DFU device is not recognised by the host PC on CN8 after mboot is installed, a\n49\treliable alternative is to program both mboot and the main MicroPython firmware directly\n50\tvia the ST-LINK interface in a single step.  This bypasses DFU mode entirely and writes\n51\tboth images to the external SPI flash using `STM32_Programmer_CLI`:\n52\t\n53\t    STM32_Programmer_CLI \\\n54\t        -c port=SWD mode=HOTPLUG ap=1 \\\n55\t        -el ${STM32_CUBE_PROGRAMMER}/bin/ExternalLoader/MX25UM51245G_STM32N6570-NUCLEO.stldr \\\n56\t        -w mboot/build-NUCLEO_N657X0/firmware-trusted.bin 0x70000000 \\\n57\t        -w build-NUCLEO_N657X0/firmware.bin 0x70080000 \\\n58\t        -hardRst\n59\t\n60\t\n61\tAdjust `<path-to-cubeprogrammer>` to match the STM32CubeProgrammer installation on\n62\tyour system.  The first `-w` programs the trusted mboot image at the start of the\n63\texternal SPI flash (0x70000000), and the second `-w` programs the main MicroPython\n64\tfirmware at 0x70080000 (512KB into the flash, immediately after mboot).\n65\t\n66\tThis approach works regardless of whether the USB DFU device enumerates correctly, and\n67\tis especially useful on Rev code B boards where the signing header version or alignment\n68\tsettings may need to be adjusted first.\n69\t"}]},"uuid":"7d1de567-92cf-48b7-94d6-91088f93099f","timestamp":"2026-08-21T15:35:44.926Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/warner/openvela/atk-dnn647-micropython/ports/stm32/boards/NUCLEO_N657X0/board.md","content":"The mboot bootloader must first be built and deployed to this board.  Make sure that\nCN9 is in position 1-2 to select STLK as the 5V power source, that JP1 is in position\n1-2 (lower position) and JP2 is in position 2-3 (upper position).  Then plug in a USB\ncable into the ST-LINK port CN10.  This will allow mboot firmware to be programmed to\nthe external SPI flash via ST's tools, eg:\n\n    make -C ports/stm32/mboot BOARD=NUCLEO_N657X0 deploy-trusted\n\nOnce mboot is installed, change CN9 to position 3-4 to select USB as the 5V power\nsource, change JP2 back to position 1-2 (lower position) and change the USB cable to\nCN8.  mboot will present a USB DFU device on this USB port, and the red LED2 should be\nblinking at 1Hz to indicate that mboot is active.  If it's not active then hold the\nUSER button and press NRST, and wait until all three LEDs are on, then release USER.\nNow mboot will be active.\n\nOnce the USB DFU port can be seen, the firmware below can be programmed as usual with\nany DFU loader.\n\n## Chip revision and signing header version\n\nThe STM32N657X0 chip is available in two silicon revisions, which require different\nsigning header versions when building the trusted firmware image.  Check the revision\ncode printed on the chip package:\n\n- **Rev code Z (Cut 1.1)**: use `STM32_N6_HEADER_VERSION = 2.1`\n- **Rev code B (Cut 2.0)**: use `STM32_N6_HEADER_VERSION = 2.3` (default in\n  `mpconfigboard.mk`)\n\nTo override the version without modifying `mpconfigboard.mk`, pass it on the make\ncommand line:\n\n    make -C ports/stm32/mboot BOARD=NUCLEO_N657X0 STM32_N6_HEADER_VERSION=2.3\n\n## STM32CubeProgrammer v2.21.0 and later\n\nStarting with STM32CubeProgrammer v2.21.0, the signing tool no longer automatically\naligns the binary payload to a 0x400-byte boundary.  The `--align` flag must be added\nexplicitly to the `STM32_SigningTool_CLI` invocation in both `ports/stm32/mboot/Makefile`\nand `ports/stm32/Makefile`.  Find the line containing `STM32_SigningTool_CLI` in each\nfile and append `--align`:\n\n    $(STM32_CUBE_PROGRAMMER)/bin/STM32_SigningTool_CLI -bin $^ -nk -of 0x80000000 -t fsbl -o $@ -hv $(STM32_N6_HEADER_VERSION) --align\n\nWithout this flag, mboot will not boot properly after programming.\n\n## Flashing mboot and firmware together via ST-LINK\n\nIf the DFU device is not recognised by the host PC on CN8 after mboot is installed, a\nreliable alternative is to program both mboot and the main MicroPython firmware directly\nvia the ST-LINK interface in a single step.  This bypasses DFU mode entirely and writes\nboth images to the external SPI flash using `STM32_Programmer_CLI`:\n\n    STM32_Programmer_CLI \\\n        -c port=SWD mode=HOTPLUG ap=1 \\\n        -el ${STM32_CUBE_PROGRAMMER}/bin/ExternalLoader/MX25UM51245G_STM32N6570-NUCLEO.stldr \\\n        -w mboot/build-NUCLEO_N657X0/firmware-trusted.bin 0x70000000 \\\n        -w build-NUCLEO_N657X0/firmware.bin 0x70080000 \\\n        -hardRst\n\n\nAdjust `<path-to-cubeprogrammer>` to match the STM32CubeProgrammer installation on\nyour system.  The first `-w` programs the trusted mboot image at the start of the\nexternal SPI flash (0x70000000), and the second `-w` programs the main MicroPython\nfirmware at 0x70080000 (512KB into the flash, immediately after mboot).\n\nThis approach works regardless of whether the USB DFU device enumerates correctly, and\nis especially useful on Rev code B boards where the signing header version or alignment\nsettings may need to be adjusted first.\n","numLines":69,"startLine":1,"totalLines":69}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"79ea3d7c-1278-4d5b-be56-3554a1a70911","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/warner/openvela","sessionId":"2d4ac909-674d-4c60-84e6-9f60f265e8c3","version":"2.1.237","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"synthetic-wandering-llama"}
